A host loads pi from its bundle, as one module graph with the durable
runtime it drives.

A host loading pi unbundled holds about 10 MB more heap a conversation
and spends about three times the CPU importing it than pi's own bundle
does, with the same exports. The bundle keeps pi-ai, pi-tui and
pi-agent-core in its own chunks and hands them to extensions from its
virtual modules, which it doesn't export; and durable, loaded beside
it, would bring its own copy of pi-ai, whose session cleanups and
UUIDv7 sequence are kept per copy. So the bundle gains a host entry,
built in the main split build so that it shares the bundle's chunks:
the virtual modules, the built-in extensions, project trust, durable's
main, SQLite and Node environment entries, and chord's context, each
from the file an unbundled host loads, with hostEntryVersion 1.

The host's own code imports some of those libraries as well, the
viewers' terminal pi-tui among them, and a second copy would split the
state they keep. So each library the virtual modules hand out under
@earendil-works/ also gets host-modules/<name>.js, re-exporting the
bundle's copy, for the host to resolve its imports to. The build fails
when the virtual modules hand out a library with no such file, or when
a durable or chord module that finds files through import.meta.url
enters the bundle.

